---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-b12`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin B12 (Cobalamin) Reference',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  about: [
    { '@type': 'MedicalCondition', name: 'Vitamin B12 deficiency', code: { '@type': 'MedicalCode', codeValue: 'E53.8', codingSystem: 'ICD-10' } },
    { '@type': 'MedicalCondition', name: 'Pernicious anaemia' },
  ],
  lastReviewed: '2026-05-01',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamins & Minerals', item: `${SITE}/vitamins` },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'Vitamin B12', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin B12 (Cobalamin): Deficiency, Sources, and Optimal Dosing"
  description="Complete evidence-based reference on vitamin B12 — pernicious anaemia, neurological deficiency, methylcobalamin vs cyanocobalamin, dosing for vegans and older adults."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamins", label: "Vitamins & Minerals" }, { href: "/vitamin-b12", label: "Vitamin B12" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin B12 (Cobalamin)</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      B12 is a cobalt-containing water-soluble vitamin that the body cannot synthesise. It is
      required for DNA synthesis, red blood cell formation, and myelin maintenance in nerves.
      Deficiency is common — up to 20% of adults over 60 have marginal or low B12 — and
      neurological damage can be irreversible if left untreated.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Daily requirements</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1">
      <li><strong>Adults:</strong> 2.4 µg/day (IOM RDA).</li>
      <li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> 2.6 µg/day.</li>
      <li><strong>Lactation:</strong> 2.8 µg/day.</li>
      <li><strong>No Tolerable Upper Intake Level</strong> — B12 has no known toxicity from oral intake.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Deficiency causes</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
      Dietary deficiency is rare in omnivores but common in strict vegans (B12 is exclusively
      animal-derived in nature). Absorption problems are more common than dietary shortage:
    </p>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Pernicious anaemia</strong> — autoimmune destruction of gastric parietal cells; loss of intrinsic factor.</li>
      <li><strong>Atrophic gastritis</strong> — age-related reduction in stomach acid; affects ~10–30% of adults ≥ 60.</li>
      <li><strong>Proton pump inhibitors and H₂ blockers</strong> — chronic acid suppression impairs cleavage of B12 from food proteins.</li>
      <li><strong>Metformin</strong> — long-term use lowers serum B12 by ~10–30%.</li>
      <li><strong>Bariatric surgery</strong> — bypass of gastric acid production.</li>
      <li><strong>Crohn's, coeliac disease, ileal resection</strong> — reduced ileal absorption.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Symptoms of deficiency</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1">
      <li>Fatigue and weakness (macrocytic anaemia)</li>
      <li>Peripheral neuropathy — numbness, tingling, burning in hands and feet</li>
      <li>Cognitive changes — brain fog, memory impairment, confusion</li>
      <li>Balance problems and gait instability (posterior column dysfunction)</li>
      <li>Glossitis (smooth, red, painful tongue)</li>
      <li>Depression and psychiatric symptoms in severe cases</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Food sources</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Clams (up to 84 µg per 3 oz), beef liver (70 µg per 3 oz), fortified nutritional yeast
      (10 µg per 2 tbsp), salmon, tuna, beef, milk, eggs. Vegans <strong>must</strong>
      supplement or reliably consume fortified foods.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Supplementation</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Cyanocobalamin</strong> — most common and stable form. 500–1,000 µg oral daily is standard for maintenance.</li>
      <li><strong>Methylcobalamin</strong> — active form, favoured by some clinicians for neuropathy. Cost is higher; efficacy is similar for most people.</li>
      <li><strong>Sublingual and injectable</strong> — used in pernicious anaemia and severe deficiency (1,000 µg IM weekly × 4, then monthly).</li>
      <li>Vegans: 1,000 µg/week or 10 µg/day covers requirements safely.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Testing</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Serum B12 is the first-line test. Normal range 200–900 pg/mL. Values below 200 pg/mL
      indicate deficiency. Between 200–400 pg/mL is a grey zone; check <strong>methylmalonic
      acid</strong> (MMA) and <strong>homocysteine</strong> — both are elevated in true
      cellular deficiency and confirm the diagnosis.
    </p>

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> Neurological B12 deficiency can be permanent if
      not treated promptly — persistent numbness, balance problems, or unexplained fatigue
      warrant testing.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-b12" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
